WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Next, we'll talk about injuries to the bones and joints.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.080
You'll often hear the words break and fracture used differently, but in the medical world,

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:13.440
they mean the exact same thing, a bone that is cracked or snapped.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.720
A sprain is slightly different.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.799
That's when you've stretched or torn a ligament, which is the soft tissue that holds your joints

00:00:19.799 --> 00:00:20.799
together.

00:00:20.799 --> 00:00:25.719
When you're assessing an injury, look for signs like bleeding, bruising, or significant

00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:26.840
swelling.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.000
The person will likely feel pain if they try to move the body part or put any weight on

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.240
it, and in more severe cases, you might see a limb that looks abnormally bent.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:42.799
If you suspect any of these, your primary treatment plan is to follow the acronym, R.I.C.E.

00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:44.200
Let's break that down.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:50.040
R stands for rest, meaning you should avoid moving the area or placing any weight on it.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.119
I is for ice.

00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:58.000
Place an ice pack on the injury for 15-20 minutes about 3-4 times a day to manage pain

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.560
and swelling.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:01.279
C is for compress.

00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:04.879
As you wrap the injury, keep it snug, but not tight.

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:09.480
If the person's skin becomes discolored or numb, loosen the wrap immediately.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:15.599
Finally, E is for elevate, where you raise the injured area above the level of the heart.

00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:20.279
It is important to remember that if a limb is deformed or abnormally bent, do not try

00:01:20.279 --> 00:01:21.680
to straighten it.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.559
Your goal is only to splint it exactly as it is to keep it from moving further.

00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:31.680
If the person is in extreme pain, or if you are ever in doubt about the severity, go ahead

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.040
and call 911.

